==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|mōrn}}, {{m|enm|morwen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|morġen}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*murganaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*murginaz}} (compare {{cog|fy|moarn}}, {{cog|nds|Morgen}}, {{cog|nl|morgen}}, {{cog|de|Morgen}}, {{cog|da|morgen}}, {{cog|no|morgon}}), from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*mr̥kéno}}, {{m|ine-pro|*mr̥kóno}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*mr̥Hko}} (compare {{cog|cy|bore|t=morning}}, {{cog|lt|mérkti|t=to blink, twinkle}}, {{cog|sa|मरीचि|tr=márīci|t=ray of light}}), from {{m|ine-pro|*mer-|t=to shimmer, glisten}} (compare {{cog|el|μέρα|sc=polytonic|t=morning}}). See also {{m|en|morrow}}, {{m|en|morning}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/mɔːn/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/mɔɹn/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɔː(ɹ)n|lang=en}}
* {{homophone|mourn|mourne|morne|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|poetic}} [[morning|Morning]].
#* [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''[[s:Hamlet|Hamlet]]'', lines 165-168,
#*: But look, the '''morn''', in russet mantle clad, / Walks o'er the dew of yon high eastward hill. / Break we our watch up, and by my advice, / Let us impart what we have seen tonight

